**Overloading** (compile-time or static polymorphism): several methods with the **same name in the same class** (or inherited) but **different parameter lists** (number, type or order). The compiler picks the method from the argument types. Return type alone cannot distinguish overloads.

**Overriding** (runtime or dynamic polymorphism): a **subclass redefines an inherited method** with the **same name and parameters**. The JVM chooses the implementation from the **actual object type** at runtime (dynamic dispatch).

| | Overloading | Overriding |
|---|---|---|
| Where | same class | subclass and superclass |
| Signature | must differ | must match |
| Binding | compile time | runtime |
| Return type | can differ | same or covariant subtype |
| Access | any | cannot be more restrictive |
| static, private, final | can be overloaded | cannot be overridden |

```java
class Animal {
    void sound() { System.out.println("..."); }
    void eat(String f) {}
    void eat(String f, int qty) {}      // overload
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override void sound() { System.out.println("Bark"); }   // override
}
Animal a = new Dog();
a.sound();   // prints Bark (decided at runtime)
```

Overriding rules: use `@Override` so the compiler checks it; an overriding method cannot throw broader checked exceptions; static methods are *hidden*, not overridden.

**Can constructors be overloaded?** Yes. A class may have several constructors with different parameters, and `this(...)` can chain them. Constructors **cannot be overridden** because they are not inherited, and a subclass constructor calls a superclass constructor with `super(...)`.

**Can `main` be overloaded?** Yes, in Java you can write other methods named `main` with different parameters, and they compile. But the JVM only starts execution from `public static void main(String[] args)`; the overloads run only if you call them.

**Python note:** Python has no compile-time overloading; a later `def` with the same name replaces the earlier one. Use default arguments, `*args` or `functools.singledispatch`. Overriding works normally through inheritance.
